# Let Git normalise text, but not for the files something other than Windows
# has to read.
#
# Git on Windows checks files out with CRLF by default. A shell script with
# CRLF fails on its first line — the interpreter named after #! arrives with a
# carriage return glued to it — and scripts/get_coastline.sh is what produces
# the coastline the pipeline cannot run without.
#
# The SNAP graphs are read inside a Linux container. XML tolerates CRLF, but
# there is no reason to hand a container Windows line endings, and the graphs
# are also rewritten by the pipeline itself when a slice holds no land.
* text=auto

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.xml  text eol=lf

# The shapefile is binary in every part. Marked explicitly, because a .prj and
# a .dbf look textual enough for a heuristic to get wrong, and a coastline
# mangled by line-ending conversion would mask the wrong pixels in silence.
*.shp binary
*.shx binary
*.dbf binary
*.prj binary
